<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baths of Caracalla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 212–<a href="/years/216">216</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Terme di Caracalla</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Baths</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a> were the second largest public bath complex of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, after the Baths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>. Probably begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> and completed by his son Caracalla, they were inaugurated in 216 CE, heated by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hypocaust">hypocaust</a> and fed by a dedicated aqueduct, and were free and open to the public. In 537, during the Gothic War, the Ostrogothic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witigis">Vitiges</a> cut Rome's water supply, and the baths were soon abandoned. Their ruins yielded sculptures such as the Farnese Bull and the Farnese Hercules, and inspired buildings from the Basilica of Maxentius to the original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> Station in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baths-of-caracalla.jpg" alt="Baths of Caracalla" width="330" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ethan Doyle White · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baths_of_Caracalla,_facing_Caldarium.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The baths stood in the southern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, on the site of a vast garden estate developed by Gaius Asinius Pollio under Augustus. Caracalla appropriated the estate, and existing structures were in some cases demolished to their ground floors and built into the foundations; one noble house built under Hadrian has been opened to the public. For the work to have been mostly complete in Caracalla's time, workers would have had to install over 2,000 tonnes of material every day for six years. Decoration continued under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elagabalus">Elagabalus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/severus-alexander">Severus Alexander</a>, and Constantine the Great modified the caldarium.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The complex covered about 25 hectares. The main building, 214 by 110 metres and 44 metres high, could hold an estimated 1,600 bathers, and it was more a leisure centre than a series of baths, with facilities for walks, reading, exercise and body care. The surrounding walls housed cisterns, two libraries for Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> texts, exedras and shops.</p><p>The central frigidarium had a roof carried by eight giant columns of Egyptian granite. The circular caldarium was topped by a dome of almost 36 metres, close to the size of the Pantheon's, carried by only eight masonry pillars with glass windows between them. Tunnels beneath held the furnaces, which are estimated to have burned about ten tons of wood a day, and a Mithraeum discovered in 1912, the largest known meeting place of the worshippers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithra</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the 5th century the baths were counted among the seven wonders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The earthquake of 847 destroyed much of the building, and from at least the 12th century it was used as a quarry, supplying materials to Pisa Cathedral and Santa Maria in Trastevere. Excavations under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Pope Paul III</a> from 1545 to 1547 uncovered many large statues. Two granite tubs from the frigidarium serve as fountains in Piazza Farnese.</p><p>The frigidarium shaped the Baths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>, the Basilica of Maxentius and, in the 19th and early 20th centuries, St George's Hall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> and the Senate of Canada Building. The Rome Opera used the caldarium as a summer home from 1937 to 1993, and the 1960 Summer Olympics held gymnastics there. The baths reopened to the public in 2001.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baths of Caracalla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
